Transition metal nitrides (TMNs) represent an important category of materials with interesting optoelectronic, catalytic, electrochemical, and structural functions.1-5 However, the synthesis of TMNs is often challenging due to the generally unreactive nature of the most common nitrogen sources, including ${\bf N}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{NH}_{3},$ at low temperature and pressure.5 Despite various methods, including atomic layer deposition and physical vapor deposition techniques, have been developed,6, 7 facile and environmentally-friendly approaches for the preparation of nanoscale TMNs with high surface areas are still highly desirable. Previously, we reported a facile “copolymer-co-morphology” conception for shape control synthesis various Prussian blue analogues (PBAs).8 Via a one-step thermal decomposition of the PBAs in air, porous spinel $\mathbf{Mn_{x}F e_{1.8-x}C o_{1.2}O_{4}}$ nano-dices and $\mathbf{Fe_{x}C o_{3}}$ - $\phantom{0}_{\phantom{4}}$ nanocages could be successfully synthesized.8, 9 The resulting composition and morphology of the spinels well-inherited the topology of metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) of PBAs precursors.10-12 Furthermore, topotactic transformation was applied as a feasible approach for fabrication of various functional nanocomposites such as monocrystalline bimetal hydroxides, porous metal phosphates and oxides etc.13-16 However, whether TMNsbased materials with well-controlled morphology could be fabricated from such strategy is still unknown.
Sulfate radical $(S O_{4}{\bullet}^{-})$ -based advanced oxidation processes (SR-AOPs) through peroxymonosulfate (PMS) activation have received increasing attention for the degradation of recalcitrant pollutants in water due to the strong oxidizing capability of $S O_{4}$ •- at a wide $\mathtt{p H}$ range.17-20 Among various transition metal catalysts, Cobalt has been proved as the most efficient PMS activator to produce $\wp_{4}\bullet$ . However, the potential carcinogenic effects of Co remarkably limit its practical application and the innovation of the catalysis for PMS activation is highly desirable.21-24
Manganese-based materials, with the unique $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}^{2+}/\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}^{3+}$ redox loop involving a single electron transfer and less toxic than cobalt to the environment, have been widely developed for applications in SR-AOPs to produce sulfate radical.25-31 It has been reported that the activity of different oxidation states of manganese for PMS activation follows the order of $\bf M n_{2}O_{3}>\bf M n O>$ $\mathrm{Mn}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}>\mathrm{MnO}_{2}.$ .28 ${\bf{\alpha}}{\bf{{\alpha}}}{\bf{{\alpha}}}{\bf{{\alpha}}}{\bf{{\alpha}}}{\bf{{\alpha}}}({\bf{{\alpha}}}{\bf{{\alpha}}}{\bf{{\alpha}}}{\bf{{\alpha}}}{\bf{{\alpha}}}{\bf{{\alpha}}}{\bf{{\alpha}}})$ cube was found present a better catalytic activity than octahedral and truncated octahedral $\mathbf{\alpha}_{\mathbf{{d}-M}\mathbf{{n}}_{2}\mathbf{{O}}_{3}},$ suggesting that shape-controlled synthesis is an efficient route to improve the catalytic performance.29 However, the activity of manganese oxide for PMS activation is usually much lower and requires a higher PMS dosage when compared to the cobalt-based catalysts. $\bf{M n}_{4}N_{.}$ , due to its face-centered (FCC) cubic cell structure with one $\mathbf{N}$ atom in the center, the Mn atoms could loss electrons to $\mathbf{N}$ ions and thus increase its reducing capacity,32 may have great significance for efficient activation of PMS to produce $\mathrm{\Delta}S{\bf O}_{4}{\bullet}^{\mathrm{\tiny~.}}$ . However, as far as we know, few reports functionalized TMNs for PMS activation in environmental applications. In addition, the PMS activation processes by nitrogen coordinated metals have not been well illustrated.
Herin, we have successfully synthesized series of graphene encapsulated TMNs $(\mathrm{Fe_{x}M n_{6-x}C o_{4}}\mathrm{-N}\varpi\mathrm{C};$ ; $0<\mathbf{x}<$ 6) with well-controlled morphology by topotactic transformation of $\mathbf{Mn}_{\mathrm{y}}\mathbf{Fe}_{1-\mathrm{y}}\lbrack\mathbf{Co}(\mathbf{CN})_{6}\rbrack_{0.67}\bullet\mathbf{n}\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ $(\mathbf{Mn}_{\mathrm{y}}\mathbf{Fe}_{1\cdot\mathrm{y}^{-}}$ Co PBAs; $0<\mathbf{y}<1$ ) in ${\bf N}_{2}$ atmosphere. The morphology, textural property and crystalline structure of the prepared $\mathbf{Fe_{x}M n_{6-x}C o_{4}-N}\boldsymbol{\mathcal{Q}}\boldsymbol{\alpha}\mathbf{C}$ were thoroughly characterized by various techniques. The series of $\mathrm{Fe_{x}M n_{6-x}C o_{4}{-N}}\varpi{\mathsf{C}}$ C nano-dices were functionalized as Fenton-like catalysts for bisphenol A (BPA) oxidation by activation of PMS. The PMS activation processes over $\mathbf{Fe_{x}M n_{6-x}C o_{4}-N_{(\overline{{\varrho}})}C}$ was systematically investigated and density functional theory (DFT) calculations were applied for further confirming the superiority of $\mathbf{Mn}_{4}\mathbf{N}$ compared to $\mathbf{MnO}$ . Both sulfate and hydroxyl radicals were identified involved and the degradation pathway of BPA was further investigated. Moreover, the overall PMS activation mechanism over $\mathbf{Fe_{x}M n_{6-x}C o_{4}-N_{(o)C}}$ was systematically studied and proposed.
## RESULTS AND DISCUSSION
The MOF precursors, $\bf{M n}_{y}\bf{F e}_{1-y}–C o$ PBAs, were prepared similarly to our previous report.8 The SEM images of the PBAs (Figure S1) reveal the well-controlled morphology of the four samples like nano-dices with good dispersity. A TG-DSC profile of $\mathbf{Mn}_{0.4}\mathbf{Fe}_{0.6^{-}}\mathbf{Co}$ PBA under ${\bf N}_{2}$ atmosphere indicates two decomposition stages (Figure S2). The weight loss at the first stage (below 215 ${}^{\mathrm{~\circ~}}\mathbf{C}$ ) could be ascribed to the loss of water molecules from the MOFs structure. The following weight loss at the second stage (over $455^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\mathrm{\cdot}$ ) could be ascribed to the conversion of MOFs structure to the final products. As illustrated in Scheme 1, through thermal decomposition of $\bf{M n_{y}F e_{1-y}-C o}$ PBAs at $650~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in ${\bf N}_{2},$ the $\mathbf{CN^{-}}$ group of PBAs will serve as nitrogen and carbon sources to form of nitrogen doped graphene layers.37-39 Meanwhile, Co and Fe atoms will form FeCo alloy while Mn atoms will form $\mathbf{Mn}_{4}\mathbf{N}$ nanocrystals inside, which was most probably due to the same crystal system (Cubic) and space group $\left(\mathrm{Pm}{\cdot}3\mathrm{m}\right)$ of FeCo and $\mathbf{Mn}_{4}\mathbf{N}$ . The final obtained $\mathbf{Fe_{x}M n_{6-x}C o_{4}{-N}}\mathbf{\widehat{\Gamma}}_{0}\mathbf{C}$ particles, constructed from the small FeCo and $\bf{M n}_{4}\bf{N}$ nanocrystals encapsulated in graphene layers, will inherited the morphology of $\bf{M n}_{y}F e_{1-y}–C o$ PBAs.
## ACS Nano
Figure 1A-D display the typical SEM images of the obtained $\mathrm{Fe_{x}M n_{6-x}C o_{4}-N}(\overline{{a}}$ C. Extremely uniform morphologies of the $\mathbf{Fe_{x}M n_{6-x}C o_{4}-N}\boldsymbol{\ @}\mathbf{C}$ particles like nano-dices, well-inherited the morphologies of $\bf{M n}_{y}\bf{F e}_{1-y}–C o$ PBAs, were obtained. In addition, with the content of Mn increasing, a regular change of the morphology of $\mathbf{Fe_{x}M n}_{6}$ - $\mathbf{\Pi}_{\mathbf{x}}\mathbf{C}\mathbf{o}_{4}{-}\mathbf{N}(\widehat{\varrho}\mathbf{C}$ nano-dices could be realized, suggesting the flexibility of the strategy for shape-control synthesis. The EDX mappings of $\mathrm{Fe_{4}M n_{2}C o_{4}\mathrm{-N_{(}}\overline{{\alpha}}C}$ are shown in Figure 1E, illustrating the Mn, Fe, and Co species were homogeneous distributed in the particles. The TEM image of $\mathbf{Fe_{4}M n_{2}C o_{4}-N}\boldsymbol{\mathcal{O}}\mathbf{C}$ further confirmed the porous structure of the nano-dices (Figure 1F). The HR-TEM images of single nanocrystals (Figure 1G-I) clearly show that the FeCo and $\mathbf{Mn_{4}N/F e_{4}N}$ nanocrystals with interlayer distances of $0.20\ \mathrm{nm}$ and $0.22\mathrm{nm}$ , respectively, were well coated by graphene shells with an interlayer distance of $0.34\mathrm{nm}$ .40 The element composition of the products was recorded by EDS and shown in Table S1. XRD was applied to confirmed the crystallographic structure and phase of the products (Figure 2B). As can be seen, a main phase of FeCo bimetal alloy phase (JCPDS No.44-1433) was observed in the pattern of $\mathbf{Fe}_{5}\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}_{1}\mathbf{Co}_{4}–\mathbf{N}\ @\mathbf{C}$ . However, with the increasing of the content of Mn, two peaks at $41.0^{\circ}$ and $47.8^{\circ}$ could be observed and the peak intensity became stronger, suggesting the existence of $\bf{M n}_{4}\bf{N}$ (JCPDS No.89-7380) and gradually appeared as a main phase in $\mathbf{Fe_{x}M n_{6-x}C o_{4}-N_{(o)}C}$ . Based on the Debye– Scherrer equation $\left(\mathrm{D}=0.89\lambda/\mathrm{~B~}\mathrm{cos}\theta\right)$ , the average crystalline size of $\mathbf{Fe_{x}M n_{6-x}C o_{4}{-N}}\mathbf{\widehat{\Gamma}}$ was estimated (Table S1), indicating that the particle of the samples was constructed by small nanocrystals with an average crystalline size of about $28\mathrm{nm}$ .
$^{57}\mathrm{Fe}$ Mössbauer spectroscopy was applied to investigate the coordination environment and oxidation state of Fe ions in the samples (Figure 2A). The four spectra were fitted mainly with two sextets, which could be assigned to two kinds of metallic irons in the FeCo alloy with different chemical environment and/or different crystallite size.41 The corresponding Mössbauer parameters are shown in Table S2. According to the isomer shift (IS) and hyperfine field $\left(B_{h f}\right)$ , the magenta doublet and the green sextets in the upper three spectra could be assigned $\mathrm{Fe_{4}N}$ .42 The $B_{h f}$ value of the green sextet in $\mathrm{Fe}_{5}\mathrm{Mn}_{1}\mathrm{Co}_{4}\mathrm{-N}_{}@\mathrm{C}_{\mathrm{\Omega}}(9.0\mathrm{\T})$ was found much smaller than the literature reported value (21.6T), which is most probably due to the substitution of the Fe by Mn.43 In addition, the high resolution XPS spectra of Co $2p_{3/2}$ and Mn $2p_{3/2}$ of $\mathbf{Fe_{x}M n_{6-x}C o_{4}-N_{(\overline{{\varrho}})\mathrm{C}}}$ are shown in Figure S3, the binding energy at $639.5\mathrm{eV}$ and $778.5\mathrm{eV}$ were corresponded to the metallic manganese and cobalt, respectively. The high resolution XPS spectra of $\mathbf{N}$ 1s of $\mathbf{Fe_{x}M n_{6-x}C o_{4}-N_{(\overline{{\alpha}})}C}$ nano-dices were fitted with three individual peaks, corresponding to nitrogen bound to the metal (Mn-N and Fe-N; 396.6 eV), pyridinic N $\left(398.4~\mathrm{eV}\right)$ , and graphitic N $(400.7\ \mathrm{~eV})$ , respectively (Figure 2C).44 All these results suggest that the graphene encapsulated TMNs ( $\mathbf{\tilde{Fe_{x}M n_{6-x}C o_{4}}{-N\textcircled{\alpha}}C})$ , with $\bf{M n}_{4}\bf{N}$
