and FeCo alloy as the main phase and well-controlled morphology could be successfully synthesized through the one-step thermal decomposition of $\bf{M n}_{y}\bf{F e}_{1-y}–C o$ PBAs.
The TG-DSC profile of the $\mathbf{Fe_{3}M n_{3}C o_{4}-N}\textcircled{\alpha}\mathbf{C}$ under air atmosphere is shown in Figure S4. The increase of the weight at the first stage $(197^{\circ}\mathbf{C}-332^{\circ}\mathbf{C})$ was due to the oxidation of $\mathbf{Fe/Co/Mn}$ to oxides. The following weight loss (over $332^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ ) could be ascribed to the combustion of graphene layers. It was interesting to see that the weight remained unchanged until the temperature reached $197^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ suggesting the good stability of the $\mathbf{Fe_{x}M n_{6-x}C o_{4}-N_{(\overline{{\alpha}})}C}$ nano-dices. In addition, considering the large specific surface areas (Figure S5) and the magnetic separation performance of all the four samples, they may have promising potential for sustainable remediation by activation of PMS.
The catalytic performance of the $\mathbf{Fe_{x}M n_{6-x}C o_{4}-N}\boldsymbol{\mathcal{O}}\mathbf{C}$ nano-dices was evaluated for BPA removal by activation of PMS. As shown in Figure 3A, less than $2\%$ of BPA could be degraded by PMS indicates the negligible radicals production through thermal activation of PMS at current reaction conditions. A control test using only $\mathbf{Fe}_{1}\mathbf{Mn}_{5}\mathbf{Co}_{4}–\mathbf{N}_{}@\mathbf{C}$ nano-dices reveals that only about $6\%$ of BPA could be adsorbed in $12\mathrm{min}$ . However, the simultaneous presence of $\mathbf{Fe_{x}M n_{6-x}C o_{4}-N_{(o)}C}$ and PMS led to a significant enhancement on the BPA removal efficiency, which could achieved $51\%$ , $74\%$ , $97\%$ , and $100\%$ in $12{\mathrm{~min}}$ catalyzed by $\mathbf{Fe}_{5}\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}_{1}\mathbf{Co}_{4}–\mathbf{N}\textcircled{\varnothing}\mathbf{C},$ $\mathbf{Fe_{4}M n_{2}C o_{4}}$ - $\mathbf{N}@\mathbf{C},$ , $\mathrm{Fe_{3}M n_{3}C o_{4}\mathrm{-N}(\overline{{{\varrho}}}C,}$ and $\mathrm{Fe_{1}M n_{5}C o_{4}\mathrm{-N}}(\varpi{\bf C},$ respectively. These results suggest that the $\mathbf{Fe_{x}M n_{6-x}C o_{4}}.$ - $\mathbf{N}@\mathbf{C}$ nano-dices are highly efficient catalysts for PMS activation to produce reactive radicals, which could be attributed mainly to coexistence of Fe, Mn, Co species in the core metals. The well developed graphene shells, on the other hand, most probably act as a protective layer thus increase the stability of the catalysts.45 As shown in Figure S6, the catalytic performance of regenerated $\mathbf{Fe}_{1}\mathbf{Mn}_{5}\mathbf{Co}_{4}–\mathbf{N}_{}@\mathbf{C}$ remained well even after four-cycle runs, suggesting the high stability of $\mathbf{Fe_{x}M n_{6-x}C o_{4}-N_{(\overline{{\alpha}})}C}$ nano-dices as Fenton-like catalysts for organic pollutants degradation. In addition, as the BPA removal efficiency could be largely enhanced with the increase of Mn content, the $\bf{M n}_{4}\bf{N}$ in $\mathbf{Fe_{x}M n_{6-x}C o_{4}-N}\boldsymbol{\ @}\mathbf{C}$ is therefore most probably the main factor for the higher activity.
To further confirm the role of $\bf{M n}_{4}N,$ $\mathbf{Fe_{1}M n_{5}C o_{4}}–$ ${\mathrm{\bf{H}}}(\varpi{\mathrm{\bf{C}}},$ with MnO and FeCo alloy as the main phase (Figure S7), was prepared using the same precursor by thermal decomposition at $650^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and kept for $^\textrm{\scriptsize1h}$ under $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ atmosphere. The FESEM images of the as-prepared $\mathbf{Fe}_{1}\mathbf{Mn}_{5}\mathbf{Co}_{4}–\mathbf{H}@\mathbf{C}$ are shown in Figure S8, indicating that the morphology of the PBAs could be also well-inherited after heating under $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ atmosphere. A first-order kinetic model46 was applied to calculate the apparent rate constant $(k)$ of BPA removal kinetics catalyzed by $\mathbf{Fe_{x}M n}_{6}$ $\mathbf{\Pi_{x}C o_{4}-N}\mathbf{\widehat{\Gamma}}\otimes\mathbf{C}$ and $\mathbf{Fe_{1}M n_{5}C o_{4}}$ -H@C nano-dices. As shown
