in Figure 3B, the values of $k$ increased from $0.055\ \mathrm{min^{-1}}$ to $0.480\ \mathrm{min^{-1}}$ with the increasing of the content of Mn in $\mathbf{Fe_{x}M n_{6-x}C o_{4}-N}\boldsymbol{\ @}\mathbf{C}$ . The optimal activity of $\mathrm{Fe_{1}M n_{5}C o_{4}\mathrm{-N}}(\varpi{\bf C},$ with a $k$ value equal to $0.480\ \mathrm{min^{-1}}$ , was much higher than that of $\mathrm{Fe_{1}M n_{5}C o_{4}\mathrm{-}H@C}$ (0.065 $\mathbf{min^{-1}}.$ ) as well as most of the recent reported catalysts (Table S3), $9,27,31,46{\cdot}49$ further confirming the crucial role of $\mathbf{Mn4N}$ for the largely enhanced PMS activation efficiency.
To investigate the activation mechanism, the involved radicals produced during the PMS activation were investigated using radical scavenger experiments. As shown in Figure S9, the reaction rate was largely decreased, when TBA, a common used radical scavenger for $\cdot\mathrm{OH},^{50}$ was added into the reaction system, indicating the •OH as one of the main reactive radicals involved. The reaction rate was further decreased when methanol, an effective radical scavenger for both •OH and $s\mathrm{O}_{4}..\mathrm{\cdot}$ , was added into the reaction system, suggesting that both $s0_{4}...$ and •OH were generated in the reaction system. The EPR/DMPO experiments were carried out to further confirm the involved radicals. As shown in Figure 3C, characteristic signals for both DMPO- $\mathrm{{}}S O_{4}{\bullet}^{\cdot}$ and DMPO•OH adducts were observed at the different reaction times.51, 52 The decrease of the relative intensity of the EPR signals at $4~\mathrm{min}$ was most probably due to the fast consumption of PMS during the reaction. All these results further reveal that both •OH and $\mathrm{{\so}_{4}\bullet^{\cdot}}$ were involved as the main reactive radicals during the PMS activation using $\mathbf{Fe_{x}M n_{6-x}C o_{4}-N_{(\mathcal{O})}C}$ as the catalyst. In addition, the amount of sulfate radical produced during the PMS activation at various time were quantified based on the quantitation of $B Q$ (Figure S10).53 Results demonstrated that at least $35.9\ \upmu\mathrm{mol}\ \mathrm{L}^{-1}\ \mathrm{SO}_{4}..^{.}$ was generated from PMS activation catalyzed by $\mathbf{Fe}_{1}\mathbf{Mn}_{5}\mathbf{Co}_{4}–\mathbf{N}_{}@\mathbf{C}$ nano-dices.
The main degradation intermediates of BPA during its degradation by $\mathrm{{}}S{\mathrm{O}}_{4}\bullet{\mathrm{{}}}$ and •OH radicals were detected by the time-dependent evolution of the HPLC diagrams (Figure 3D) and identified by analyzing the $m/z$ data obtained by LC-MS (Figures. S11,12). The main intermediate (product I), with a retention time (RT) of 6.95 min and $m/z$ of 242, was produced through hydroxylation and oxidative of BPA.54 As can be seen, product I was accumulated at first and then decomposed and gradually formed product I (RT 5.46), further confirming that the BPA degradation pathway was mainly through hydroxylation, oxidation and decarboxylation of the aromatic ring.9, 55 Furthermore, as shown in Figure S13, more than $43\%$ of BPA was mineralized within 12 min, suggesting that BPA was not only decomposed to small organic compounds, but even mineralized to $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ .
To better understand the effect for PMS activation on $\mathbf{MnO}$ and $\bf{M n}_{4}\bf{N}_{.}$ , density functional theory (DFT) calculations are performed to investigate the adsorption of PMS on MnO and $\mathbf{Mn}_{4}\mathbf{N}$ surfaces. Based on experimental results, MnO (200) and $\bf{M n}_{4}\bf{N}$ (111) are chosen in the calculations. After considering all the possible adsorption sites and orientation of PMS, Figure 4 shows the relaxed atomic structures of a PMS molecule adsorption on MnO (200) [panel (a)] and $\bf{M n}_{4}\bf{N}$ (111) surfaces [panel (b)], respectively. For the adsorption on MnO (200) surface, it shows that the PMS is standing on the MnO (200) surface with the two $^{\circ}$ atoms on $-S O_{4}$ side bonding with two Mn atoms on surface. The two bond lengths are 2.094, $2.277\mathring{\mathbf{A}}$ , respectively. For the adsorption of PMS on the $\bf{M n}_{4}\bf{N}$ (111) surface, the PMS is laying down with the OH group on the left, and two O atoms from $-S{\bf O}_{4}$ group bind with two Mn atoms on the surface. To better understand the interaction between the surfaces and PMS, also for the activation of PMS, Table S4 provides the adsorption energy of PMS $\left(E_{\mathrm{ads}}\right)$ on the two different surfaces, charge transfer between PMS and surface $\ Q$ and the bond length lO-O between - OH group and $-S{\bf O}_{4}$ group. The adsorption energy (or binding energy for this case) $E_{\mathrm{ads}}$ can be determined by $E_{\mathrm{ads}}=E_{\mathrm{tot}}-E_{\mathrm{MnO}}-E_{\mathrm{PMS}_{\mathrm{I}}}$ , where $E_{\mathrm{tot}},$ $E_{\mathrm{MnO;}}$ , and $E_{\mathrm{PMS}}$ are the energies of the $\mathbf{MnO}$ surface with PMS adsorption, the $\mathbf{MnO}$ surface, and the PMS molecule, respectively. As shown in Table S4, the adsorption on both surface is quite strong with $E_{\mathrm{ads}}$ being $\mathbf{-4.0}$ and $-5.23\ \mathrm{eV}$ for MnO (200) and $\bf{M n}_{4}\bf{N}$ (111) surfaces, respectively. This also agrees with the formation of covalent bonds between PMS and the two surfaces as shown in Figure 4. Comparing the two surfaces, the adsorption of PMS on $\bf{M n}_{4}\bf{N}$ (111) is stronger with stronger $E_{\mathrm{ads}},$ longer lO-O, and more electrons received on PMS. Therefore, the PMS on the $\bf{M n}_{4}\bf{N}$ (111) is more active.
